# Adapters ship as executable scripts and CI configs installed directly into
# other projects' .git/hooks/ or .github/workflows/. A CRLF line ending breaks
# a shebang line on Linux/CI even though the file still "looks fine" on
# Windows editors. Force LF for everything under Adapters/, regardless of the
# checkout platform or user's core.autocrlf setting.
/Adapters/** text eol=lf

# Registry/ is parsed by those same shell consumers with grep and sed (see
# Registry/README.md's parsing contract). A CRLF checkout would leave a
# trailing carriage return on every extracted Employee name, so `grep -qxF`
# would match nothing and the commit-msg hook would reject every valid
# Employee. The consumers strip \r defensively as well, but the file itself
# must not be the thing that introduces it.
/Registry/** text eol=lf

# .github/ holds this repository's own installed copies of the Adapters above,
# and the yaml-lint job compares them against the Adapters/ originals. Keeping
# both sides LF means a diff between them reflects real content drift rather
# than a line-ending difference on a Windows checkout.
/.github/** text eol=lf

# THE INSTALLED COPIES ARE DERIVED, AND ARE MARKED AS SUCH.
#
# install.sh writes every file below from its Adapters/ original, and section
# 10 of validate-framework.sh refuses a merge where the two differ by a single
# byte. Adapters/ is the source; this repository is simply its own first
# adopter, and these are the artefacts of that installation.
#
# Marking them `linguist-generated` does two things. The hosting platform
# collapses them in the diff view, which is right: reading the same bytes twice
# is not review. And PR Size stops counting them, which fixes a measurement
# error rather than papering over one — Standards/PULL_REQUEST_STANDARD.md puts
# the limit on "the lines a reviewer must READ", and a file byte-identical to
# another file in the same diff is not read twice. Measured on the change set
# that exposed this: 1,274 of 2,618 lines were the same 637 counted once per
# copy, and the check refused a change that was near the limit rather than 75%
# over it.
#
# THE RISK THIS WOULD NORMALLY CARRY IS ALREADY CLOSED BY ANOTHER CONTROL.
# Collapsing a file usually means a reviewer can miss a change inside it; here
# a divergence between the two copies cannot merge at all, because the parity
# check is a required status check. The exclusion is safe precisely because
# something else already guarantees what the reviewer would have been looking
# for.
#
# Enumerated one path at a time rather than as `/.github/**`. A glob would also
# swallow a file that is NOT installed from Adapters/ — a repository-specific
# workflow added later — and quietly stop counting a change nobody derived.
/.github/workflows/pr-validation.yml         linguist-generated=true
/.github/workflows/framework-validation.yml  linguist-generated=true
/.github/workflows/data-policy.yml           linguist-generated=true
/.github/workflows/control-path-guard.yml    linguist-generated=true
/.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md            linguist-generated=true
/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md        linguist-generated=true
/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/feature_request.md   linguist-generated=true
/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/config.yml           linguist-generated=true
